var tafsirData_13 = {
  "1": "\"Alif-Lãm-Mĩm-Ra\" opens this chapter with four disconnected letters, recited individually. Several chapters begin with such letters, and the position held by many early scholars is that their full meaning rests with Allah. A commonly noted observation is that these are the same letters the Arabs used in their poetry and speech, placed at the head of a Book they were challenged to match and could not.\n\n\"These are the verses of the Book.\" identifies what follows. The definite article carries weight here, pointing to the Book that was promised and expected rather than one book among many.\n\n\"What has been revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺ from your Lord is the truth\" states the central claim. Notice the description of the source: from your Lord, using the word \"Rabb,\" which indicates the One who owns, sustains, and nurtures. The revelation is not presented as a distant decree from an indifferent power but as guidance from the One who has been caring for the recipient all along.\n\nThe Arabic word for truth here, \"al-haqq,\" means what is real, established, and rightly so. It is the opposite of what is false, invented, or baseless, and the preceding chapters have spent a great deal of effort showing that the alternative to revelation is guesswork.\n\n\"But most people do not believe.\" is the sober conclusion, and it repeats a theme running through these chapters. The previous chapter ended by noting that most people will not believe no matter how eager the Prophet was for them.\n\nThe placement of this at the very opening is worth noticing. Before the chapter presents a single argument or sign, it tells the reader what the general response will be. This prevents the reader from measuring truth by popularity, which is exactly the mistake the Quran keeps warning against.\n\nWhat follows in this chapter is a sustained appeal to observation: the sky without pillars, the sun and moon in their courses, the earth with its mountains and rivers, thunder and lightning, and the growth of different fruits from the same water.",
  "2": "\"It is Allah Who has raised the heavens without pillars—as you can see—\" begins the chapter's argument from creation, and it starts by directing attention upward.\n\nThe phrase \"as you can see\" is an invitation to check. Look up: there is nothing holding it. Commentators have read the Arabic in two ways, either that there are no pillars at all, or that there are supports which you cannot see. Both readings point to the same conclusion, that something vast is being held in place by no visible means.\n\nModern knowledge deepens rather than removes the wonder. Gravity, orbital mechanics, and the balance of forces are the means, and none of them is visible to a person looking at the sky.\n\n\"Then established Himself on the Throne.\" is a statement Muslims accept as revealed, affirming it without asking how and without imagining any physical form, since nothing resembles Allah. Early scholars, including Imam Malik, gave the well-known answer that the establishment is known, its manner is unknown, believing in it is obligatory, and asking about its how is an innovation.\n\n\"He has subjected the sun and the moon, each orbiting for an appointed term.\" moves to the two bodies that govern human timekeeping. Both are described as running to a fixed term, which indicates both their regular courses and the eventual end of the arrangement.\n\n\"He conducts the whole affair.\" states who is running it. The Arabic indicates directing, managing, and arranging matters continuously. This is not a universe that was set going and left alone.\n\n\"He makes the signs clear so that you may be certain of the meeting with your Lord.\" gives the purpose of the whole display, and it is the key to the verse. The sky, the sun, and the moon are not mentioned to produce admiration. They are mentioned as evidence for resurrection. The One who raised the heavens and set the sun in its course can certainly gather people again.",
  "3": "\"And He is the One Who spread out the earth\" brings the argument down from the sky to the ground underfoot. The Arabic indicates extending and spreading something out, making it broad and usable.\n\nThis description has sometimes been misread as a claim that the earth is flat. The word describes how the earth functions for those living on it, which is exactly what a person experiences: a surface stretched out and habitable. A very large sphere is, for its inhabitants, spread out. The Quran elsewhere uses expressions indicating that the earth was made a resting place and a bed, all of which describe usability rather than geometry.\n\n\"And placed firm mountains and rivers upon it\" names two features and pairs them deliberately. Mountains are described elsewhere in the Quran as stabilisers, and modern geology describes their deep roots extending far below the surface. Rivers are the arteries that make settled life possible.\n\nThe pairing is functional. Mountains catch and store water as snow and ice, and rivers carry it down to the plains where crops grow. The two are a single system.\n\n\"And created fruits of every kind in pairs\" points to something the Arabs would not have known in botanical terms. Plants reproduce through male and female elements, a fact established scientifically many centuries after this was recited. Commentators before that discovery understood the phrase as referring to varieties and matched types, which is also a valid reading of the Arabic. The wording accommodates both, and the later knowledge does not contradict it.\n\n\"He covers the day with night.\" describes the daily transition using an image of one thing wrapping over another, which matches how darkness moves across a rotating surface.\n\n\"Surely in this are signs for those who reflect.\" names the audience once again, and the Arabic indicates people who think matters through deliberately. The evidence is placed in the ordinary world, and it works only on someone willing to stop and consider it.",
  "4": "\"And on the earth there are ˹different˺ neighbouring tracts\" begins an argument built on something farmers notice constantly. Pieces of land lying side by side, sharing the same climate and the same rain, produce different results.\n\nThe observation is precise. These are adjoining plots, not distant regions with different conditions. One field is fertile and the next is poor, and no simple explanation accounts for it.\n\n\"Gardens of grapevines, ˹various˺ crops, palm trees—some stemming from the same root, others standing alone\" adds detail from the agricultural world of the region. The mention of palms growing in clusters from one root and others standing single is a specific horticultural fact, the kind of detail a farmer would recognise immediately.\n\n\"They are all irrigated with the same water, yet We make some taste better than others.\" is the heart of the argument, and it is genuinely striking when considered carefully.\n\nThe same water, the same soil in adjacent plots, the same sun, and the results differ in taste, size, sweetness, and quality. Two dates from the same grove can be entirely unlike each other. If the outcome were simply the product of physical inputs, identical inputs would produce identical outputs. They do not.\n\nThis points toward an arrangement rather than an accident. Something is determining these differences, and the Quran attributes it directly: We make some better than others.\n\nThere is a further layer that commentators have drawn out. The same argument applies to human beings. People receive the same revelation, hear the same message, and grow in the same community, and they turn out completely different. The soil of the heart determines what the same water produces, which is a comparison the Prophet made explicitly in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim, likening the guidance he brought to rain falling on different kinds of ground.\n\n\"Surely in this are signs for those who understand.\" closes with an appeal to intelligence, using a word that indicates reasoning things out.",
  "5": "\"˹Now,˺ if anything should amaze you ˹O Prophet˺, then it is their question: \"When we are reduced to dust, will we really be raised as a new creation?\"\" identifies what deserves astonishment, and it reverses the direction they intended.\n\nThey thought resurrection was the absurd claim. The verse says their question is the astonishing thing.\n\nThe reasoning behind that reversal has just been laid out in the preceding verses. A person who accepts that the heavens are held up without visible support, that the sun and moon run on fixed courses, and that identical water produces different fruits, has already accepted power far beyond what resurrection requires. Rebuilding something is easier than making it the first time, by any human measure.\n\nThe Quran presses this argument in several places, most memorably in Surah Yasin, where a man holds up a decayed bone and asks who will revive it, and the answer given is that the One who produced it the first time will do so, since He knows every creation.\n\n\"It is they who have disbelieved in their Lord.\" names what the question actually expresses. It is not a scientific objection but a refusal, and the description uses the word \"Lord,\" the One who has sustained them throughout the very life they think cannot be repeated.\n\n\"It is they who will have shackles around their necks.\" describes their end with an image of restraint and humiliation. A neck held in irons is the posture of a captive with no ability to move or resist.\n\nThere is an appropriateness in the image worth noticing. They had refused to bow willingly, and the shackle forces the neck into a bent position.\n\n\"And it is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever.\" completes the outcome. The repetition of \"it is they\" three times in the verse hammers the identification: the ones asking the mocking question are the ones described here.",
  "6": "\"They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ to hasten the torment rather than grace\" describes a demand that is difficult to understand until one recognises the attitude behind it.\n\nGiven a choice between asking for good and asking for punishment, they chose punishment. Not because they wanted it, but because they were certain it would never arrive, and demanding it seemed the quickest way to expose the Prophet as false.\n\nThe Arabic word for the good they might have asked for indicates a fine, beautiful thing, and it is set directly against the punishment they requested instead.\n\n\"Though there have ˹already˺ been ˹many˺ torments before them.\" points out why the demand is foolish. Examples exist. The peoples of Noah, Ad, Thamud, Lut, and Madyan were all described in detail two chapters earlier, and their ruins lay along the trade routes the Meccans used.\n\nSomeone asking for a demonstration of divine punishment is asking for something that has already been demonstrated repeatedly.\n\n\"Surely your Lord is full of forgiveness for people, despite their wrongdoing\" explains why they were still standing. The delay they mistook for absence was forgiveness in operation. Surah an-Nahl states the principle plainly: if Allah were to punish people for their wrongdoing, He would not leave a single creature on earth.\n\nThe wording is careful. Forgiveness is described as being extended to people despite their wrongdoing, which is precisely what makes it forgiveness rather than mere tolerance.\n\n\"And your Lord is truly severe in punishment.\" completes the balance in the same breath.\n\nThe Quran consistently refuses to let either half stand alone. A God described only as forgiving would make warnings meaningless; a God described only as severe would make repentance pointless. The Prophet expressed this balance in a hadith recorded by Muslim, saying that if a believer knew what punishment Allah has, no one would hope for His Paradise, and if a disbeliever knew what mercy He has, no one would despair of it.",
  "7": "\"The disbelievers say, \"If only a sign could be sent down to him from his Lord.\"\" records a demand the Meccans made repeatedly, and it needs to be understood correctly.\n\nThey were not asking for evidence in general. They had evidence: the Quran itself, the character of the man delivering it, and the signs in creation the previous verses had just described. What they wanted was a spectacular miracle of their own choosing.\n\nOther passages record the specifics: a spring gushing from the ground, a house of gold, a garden with rivers running through it, angels descending in visible form, or a book they could hold and read. The Quran answers these demands in several places, noting that earlier peoples were given exactly such signs and destroyed when they rejected them.\n\nThat is the danger in the demand. A sign given on request removes the space in which faith operates, and the pattern in earlier communities was that refusal after such a sign brought immediate destruction. Withholding it was mercy.\n\n\"You ˹O Prophet˺ are only a warner.\" returns him to his actual role. He does not produce miracles on demand, and he is not required to satisfy every request. His task is to warn clearly, which he had done.\n\n\"And every people had a guide.\" is the second half, and commentators have read it in two complementary ways. It may mean that every community in history was sent someone to guide it, which the Quran states elsewhere when it says there is no community that has not had a warner. Or it may mean that for every people there is a guide, referring to the Prophet for his own community.\n\nEither reading answers the demand for a sign. Guidance has never been withheld from any people. What varies is whether they accept the guide who comes or spend their time specifying the terms on which they might consider it.",
  "8": "\"Allah knows what every female bears\" opens a passage about knowledge, and it begins with something that was entirely hidden when these words were first recited.\n\nFor most of human history, what a woman carried was unknown until birth. The sex of the child, its health, whether it was one or more, all of it lay beyond anyone's reach. The verse points to a domain of complete human ignorance and states that Allah's knowledge covers it.\n\nModern medicine has changed part of this, and it is worth being honest about that rather than pretending otherwise. Ultrasound reveals a great deal. What it does not reveal is the whole of what the verse indicates: the child's future, character, lifespan, and everything that will follow from that beginning. The Prophet mentioned matters known only to Allah in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, and the general knowledge of what is in the wombs is among them, understood by scholars as knowledge of the complete reality rather than a physical scan.\n\n\"And what increases and decreases in the wombs\" adds a further layer. Commentators explained this as covering variations in the length of pregnancy, the growth or shrinking of what is carried, and the differences that occur from one case to another. Some understood it as referring to pregnancies that end early and those that extend beyond the usual term.\n\n\"And with Him everything is determined with precision.\" states the principle behind all of it. The Arabic indicates a measure, an exact amount assigned to each thing.\n\nThis is one of the Quran's recurring themes. Nothing in creation is arbitrary or approximate. The size of a child, the length of a life, the timing of an event, all fall within an assigned measure.\n\nFor a believer, this knowledge changes how uncertainty feels. What is unknown to us is not unknown, and what appears random has a measure attached that we simply cannot see.",
  "9": "\"˹He is the˺ Knower of the seen and the unseen\" completes the description begun in the previous verse by extending it to everything that exists.\n\nTwo categories are named, and together they leave nothing out. The Arabic \"shahadah\" refers to what is witnessed, present, and observable. \"Ghayb\" refers to what is hidden from perception, including the future, the distant past, and everything happening beyond anyone's awareness.\n\nHuman knowledge operates entirely inside the first category, and only a small portion of it. We know what we have seen, measured, or been reliably told. Everything else is inference or guesswork, which the earlier chapters identified as the root of so much false belief.\n\nFor Allah there is no such division in terms of access. Both categories are equally known, which is a difficult idea for a mind that experiences knowledge as something acquired piece by piece.\n\n\"The All-Great, Most Exalted.\" adds two names that follow naturally from such knowledge. The Arabic \"al-Kabir\" indicates greatness beyond measure, and \"al-Muta'al\" indicates being high above and beyond everything, transcendent.\n\nThe second name has a particular relevance in this chapter, which has been answering people who denied resurrection and demanded miracles on their own terms. He is above their objections, above their standards, and above being measured by human expectations.\n\nThere is a practical effect to holding this belief seriously, and it is one the Quran keeps returning to. A person who genuinely believes that the unseen is fully known behaves differently in private than someone who believes only in observed accountability. Most human ethics depend on being watched. This belief removes the possibility of not being watched.\n\nIt also changes how a person carries sorrow. Grief that nobody notices, effort that nobody records, and intentions that nobody could guess are all fully known. Jacob's statement in the previous chapter, that he complains only to Allah and knows from Allah what others do not, rests on exactly this.",
  "10": "\"It is the same ˹to Him˺ whether any of you speaks secretly or openly\" applies the knowledge described in the previous verses to ordinary human behaviour, and it does so with examples anyone can test against their own life.\n\nA whisper and a shout are entirely different to us. One reaches a single ear; the other fills a room. The verse says the distinction does not exist at the level being described. Both are equally known, with no effort involved in perceiving either.\n\nThis is not merely a statement about hearing. It reaches what a person says quietly to a trusted friend, what he mutters when nobody is present, and what he says only inside his own head.\n\n\"Whether one hides in the darkness of night or goes about in broad daylight\" gives the second example, and it addresses the deepest human instinct about concealment.\n\nDarkness feels like cover. Children believe it, and adults behave as though they do. Much wrongdoing is arranged for the night, and much of what people are ashamed of happens where no one can see. The verse states plainly that night provides no cover whatsoever.\n\nThe two examples together cover speech and action, and the private and public forms of each. Nothing is left in shadow.\n\nThe previous chapter opened with a related image, describing people who fold themselves up trying to hide from Allah, and pointing out that even when they cover themselves with their clothes, He knows what they conceal and what they reveal.\n\nFor a person of faith this belief has two effects, and both matter. It produces care about private conduct, since there is no unobserved space in which to be a different person. And it produces comfort, because the good done quietly, the patience nobody witnessed, and the sincerity that could never be proved to anyone are all recorded exactly as they were.",
  "11": "\"For each one there are successive angels before and behind, protecting them by Allah's command.\" describes something happening around every person constantly and invisibly.\n\nAngels are assigned to each human being, coming in succession, positioned in front and behind. Commentators explained their function as both protection and recording, and the Quran mentions elsewhere the two honourable scribes who write down what a person does.\n\nThe protection is described as being by Allah's command, which prevents a misunderstanding. The angels are not independent guardians who shield a person from a fate Allah has decreed. They act under instruction, and what they protect from is what He has permitted them to prevent.\n\n\"Indeed, Allah would never change a people's state ˹of favour˺ until they change their own state ˹of faith˺.\" is one of the most frequently quoted sentences in the Quran, and it deserves careful handling because it is also frequently misapplied.\n\nThe principle is that collective conditions follow collective character. A community's outward situation is connected to its inward state, and lasting change in the first requires change in the second.\n\nThis works in both directions. A people in a good state who abandon what earned it will lose it. A people in a poor state who repair their condition will find it changed. The verse is not a promise of automatic worldly success for the pious, since the Quran and the Sunnah are full of righteous people who suffered.\n\nWhat it rules out is the expectation of change without effort or reform. Complaining about a community's situation while refusing to address what produced it contradicts the principle directly.\n\n\"And if it is Allah's Will to torment a people, it can never be averted, nor can they find a protector other than Him.\" closes the verse by placing the ultimate decision where it belongs. Human effort operates genuinely, and it operates inside a larger will that nothing overrides.",
  "12": "\"He is the One Who shows you lightning, inspiring ˹you with˺ hope and fear\" turns from angels and destiny to something everyone has watched from a window.\n\nLightning produces two reactions at once, and the verse names both. Hope, because in a dry land a storm means rain, crops, and survival. Fear, because a strike can kill a man, burn a tent, or destroy a tree.\n\nThe same flash carries both meanings, and which one a person feels depends on where he is standing. A farmer with parched fields sees relief. A traveller in an open plain sees danger.\n\nThere is something in this pairing that reaches beyond weather. The Quran often describes the believer's relationship with Allah as combining hope and fear, and lightning is a natural image for it: the same reality inspires both, and neither cancels the other.\n\n\"And produces heavy clouds.\" moves from the flash to the mass of water suspended above.\n\nThe Arabic word describes clouds heavy with water, weighed down by their load. This is an accurate observation with real force behind it. A single storm cloud carries an enormous weight of water, lifted from oceans and lakes by evaporation and held in the air until it is released.\n\nThe people first hearing this had no scientific account of the water cycle, and they did not need one to feel the wonder. Water rises invisibly from the sea, gathers into visible masses, travels over land, and falls where there was nothing before.\n\nThe Quran returns to rain constantly as evidence, and the reason is straightforward. It is the clearest daily demonstration of life being produced from what is dead, which is exactly the point at issue with people who denied resurrection. Dead earth becomes green after rain, in front of everyone, every year.\n\nFor an audience demanding a miracle, the chapter keeps pointing at the sky and asking what they think they are looking at.",
  "13": "\"The thunder glorifies His praises, as do the angels in awe of Him.\" attributes worship to something people usually experience only as noise.\n\nThe Quran states in several places that everything in the heavens and the earth glorifies Allah, and Surah al-Isra adds that there is nothing which does not celebrate His praise, though people do not understand their glorification. Thunder is named here as one participant in that universal praise.\n\nCommentators have differed over how to understand this. Many took it as the sound itself glorifying Allah in a manner beyond our perception. Others mentioned reports describing an angel assigned to the clouds, and such reports exist in the commentary literature with varying degrees of strength. The safest position is to affirm what the verse says without insisting on details that are not established.\n\nPairing thunder with the angels is striking. The angels are described as being in awe of Him, using an Arabic word that indicates fear mixed with reverence. Beings of immense power and complete obedience stand in awe, and a storm participates in the same act.\n\n\"He sends thunderbolts, striking with them whoever He wills.\" adds the destructive aspect. Lightning kills, and the verse attributes it directly rather than treating it as a random natural event. Nothing occurs outside His will, including the strike that finds one man and leaves another.\n\n\"Yet they dispute about Allah.\" is the sudden turn, and it lands hard. In the middle of a description of thunder praising Him and angels standing in awe, human beings are shown arguing.\n\nThe contrast is the whole point. Creation submits, angels revere, and the one creature given the capacity to reason uses it to dispute about the One who made all of it.\n\n\"And He is tremendous in might.\" closes the verse with a reminder of who is being argued with. The Arabic indicates overwhelming strength and severity in seizing, and it is placed exactly where a person disputing might pause to reconsider.",
  "14": "\"Calling upon Him ˹alone˺ is the truth.\" states the conclusion that the whole preceding argument has been building toward. After the sky held without pillars, the sun and moon in their courses, the rain, the thunder, and the lightning, the practical point arrives: this is the One to call upon.\n\nThe Arabic word for calling here, \"du'a,\" means supplication, invoking someone for help. The Prophet described it in a hadith recorded by at-Tirmidhi as being worship itself, because calling on someone in need is an admission that he can hear, respond, and act.\n\nDescribing this as the truth means it corresponds to reality. Allah genuinely hears and genuinely responds, so calling on Him is a rational act rather than a ritual gesture.\n\n\"But those ˹idols˺ the pagans invoke besides Him ˹can˺ never respond to them in any way.\" states the alternative plainly. Nothing comes back. Not a partial answer, not an occasional one, but nothing at all.\n\n\"˹It is˺ just like someone who stretches out their hands to water, ˹asking it˺ to reach their mouths, but it can never do so.\" is the comparison, and it is worth picturing carefully.\n\nA thirsty man sits beside a well. He stretches his hands toward the water and calls to it, asking it to rise to his lips. The water is real, the thirst is real, and the gesture is real. Nothing happens, because water does not respond to being called.\n\nThe image captures the futility exactly. The man is not lazy or insincere; he is misdirected. Effort spent in the wrong direction produces nothing regardless of how much of it there is.\n\nCommentators have also noted a second layer in the picture. The man is dying of thirst beside a well that would save him if he simply reached in. Everything he needs is available, and he is calling instead of drinking.\n\n\"The calls of the disbelievers are only in vain.\" closes the verse with the word for straying: their calling wanders off, going nowhere.",
  "15": "\"To Allah ˹alone˺ bow down ˹in submission˺ all those in the heavens and the earth—willingly or unwillingly—\" describes a submission that includes everything and everyone, though not in the same manner.\n\nThe Arabic verb indicates prostrating, and here it covers the whole range of submission to Allah's power and command.\n\nThe division into willing and unwilling is the key to the verse. Some submit by choice: believers who worship deliberately, angels who obey without deviation, and creation that has no capacity to refuse. Others submit without choosing to. The most defiant person alive still ages, sickens, sleeps, and dies according to a design he did not set and cannot override. His body obeys laws he never agreed to.\n\nThat is what unwilling submission means. Nobody escapes the arrangement; the only question is whether a person's inner life is aligned with what his existence is doing anyway.\n\n\"As do their shadows, morning and evening.\" adds an image of remarkable subtlety.\n\nA shadow has no will of its own. It stretches and shortens according to the sun's position, lengthening at dawn and again toward evening, and it does this without any decision. Watch a shadow through a day and you are watching something that follows a law absolutely, with no possibility of rebellion.\n\nThe Quran uses this because everyone has seen it and nobody has thought about it. A person who insists on his independence casts a shadow that submits perfectly.\n\nThere is something quietly humbling in the choice of example. Not the mountains, not the sea, but the dark shape a man's own body makes on the ground, moving exactly as it is directed while he argues that he answers to no one.\n\nThe verse is a picture of the whole creation in one posture, with human beings the only part debating whether to join.",
  "16": "\"\"Who is the Lord of the heavens and the earth?\" is a question the Prophet is told to put to them, and the answer he is told to give immediately afterwards reveals why.\n\n\"Say, \"Allah!\"\" supplies the answer without waiting for theirs. Commentators explain this as reflecting that the answer was undeniable. The Meccans themselves acknowledged Allah as creator and sustainer, as the Quran records repeatedly. They could not dispute it, so the Prophet states it for them.\n\n\"Why ˹then˺ have you taken besides Him lords who cannot even benefit or protect themselves?\" is the real question, and it exposes the contradiction at the centre of their religion. They agreed about who made everything, and then directed their worship elsewhere.\n\nThe description of these lords is devastating in its simplicity. They cannot help themselves, let alone anyone else. An object that cannot move when it is knocked over is being asked for rain and protection.\n\n\"Can the blind and the sighted be equal? Or can darkness and light be equal?\" are two comparisons offered as tests of judgement. Nobody hesitates over either. The pairs are set out so that the answer arrives before any argument is needed, and the same method appeared in Surah Hud when the two parties were compared to the blind and the deaf against the seeing and the hearing.\n\n\"Or have they associated with Allah partners who ˹supposedly˺ produced a creation like His, leaving them confused between the two creations?\" puts the only argument that could justify their position and shows that it does not exist.\n\nIf their gods had created something comparable, there might be genuine confusion about who was responsible for what. No such claim could be made. They never said their idols created anything.\n\n\"Say, \"Allah is the Creator of all things, and He is the One, the Supreme.\"\" closes the matter with the two names Joseph used to the prisoners: the One, and the Irresistible.",
  "17": "\"He sends down rain from the sky, causing the valleys to flow, each according to its capacity.\" begins an extended comparison that is one of the finest passages in the Quran, and every element of it carries meaning.\n\nRain falls evenly, and the valleys carry different amounts. A large valley takes a great volume; a small one takes little. The water is the same, and the capacity differs. Commentators have long read this as an image of revelation reaching different hearts, each receiving according to what it can hold.\n\n\"The currents then carry along rising foam\" introduces the second element. Flowing water throws up scum on its surface, and anyone who has watched a flood knows the sight: a layer of froth and debris riding on top, highly visible, occupying the eye.\n\n\"Similar to the slag produced from metal that people melt in the fire for ornaments or tools.\" adds a second image from a completely different field. Smelting metal produces dross that rises to the top and is skimmed away. Two processes, one natural and one industrial, produce the same phenomenon: worthless material floating conspicuously above what is valuable.\n\n\"This is how Allah compares truth to falsehood.\" names the point of both images.\n\nFalsehood behaves exactly like foam. It sits on the surface, it is loud and visible, it appears to be the main thing, and it has no substance. Truth is the water underneath, quieter and less noticeable, and it is what everything actually depends on.\n\n\"The ˹worthless˺ residue is then cast away, but what benefits people remains on the earth.\" describes the outcome. Foam dries and vanishes; water soaks into the ground and grows crops. Slag is discarded; the metal becomes a ring or a blade.\n\nThe comparison answers a question believers ask in every generation. Why does falsehood appear so dominant? Because foam always looks like more than it is, and because the test is not what floats but what remains.",
  "18": "\"Those who respond to ˹the call of˺ their Lord will have the finest reward.\" divides humanity by a single act: responding.\n\nThe Arabic indicates answering a call, and it suits the passage precisely. Everything before this has been an invitation, from the sky and the rain and the growing fruit to the direct question about who created the heavens. A call has been issued, and people either answer or do not.\n\nThe reward is described with a word indicating the best and most beautiful outcome. The Quran elsewhere expands on it, mentioning that those who do good will have the best reward and more besides, which the Prophet explained, in a hadith recorded by Muslim, as the vision of Allah's face.\n\n\"As for those who do not respond to Him, even if they were to possess everything in the world twice over, they would certainly offer it to ransom themselves.\" describes the other group at the moment of realisation.\n\nThe image is deliberately extreme. Not a fortune, but everything on earth, and then the same amount again, offered instantly as payment. The offer would not be accepted, because wealth was never the currency of that court. Surah Yunus made the same point about wrongdoers who would ransom themselves with everything in the world, and Surah Ali Imran states that an earth full of gold would not be taken from those who die in disbelief.\n\nThere is a comparison worth making between the two halves of this verse. One group is offered the finest reward for something that costs nothing: answering a call. The other would give two worlds to escape, and cannot.\n\n\"They will face strict judgment, and Hell will be their home.\" completes the outcome. The Arabic indicates a severe reckoning, examined closely rather than passed over.\n\n\"What an evil place to rest!\" closes with a phrase that carries bitter irony, since the word suggests a resting place, the destination where a traveller finally settles.",
  "19": "\"Can the one who knows that your Lord's revelation to you ˹O Prophet˺ is the truth be like the one who is blind?\" returns to the comparison used a few verses earlier, and it asks the question rather than stating the conclusion.\n\nThe two people described are not distinguished by intelligence, wealth, or ability. One knows, and one is blind. The knowing here is not merely holding an opinion; the Arabic indicates recognising something as true, which is what happens when a person examines evidence honestly and sees where it points.\n\nThe blindness, as this chapter and the ones before it have made clear, is not physical. Surah al-Hajj states plainly that it is not the eyes that turn blind but the hearts within the chests.\n\nThe question format matters. Rather than announcing that the two are unequal, the verse invites the listener to say it himself. Nobody would claim that sight and blindness are equivalent, and the point transfers.\n\n\"None will be mindful ˹of this˺ except people of reason.\" identifies who actually benefits from the comparison. The Arabic term indicates those possessing sound intellect, people who use the faculty they were given.\n\nThere is something worth noticing in how consistently this chapter attaches its arguments to thinking. Signs are for those who reflect, for those who understand, and now the lesson is for people of reason. The Quran is not asking for belief in place of thought; it is asking for thought, and expecting a particular result from it.\n\nThe word for being mindful indicates remembering and taking heed, which suggests that the truth being pointed at is not entirely foreign to a person. Something in him already recognises it when it is presented honestly.\n\nWhat follows in the next verses is a detailed description of these people of reason, and it is worth noting in advance that the description is entirely about character and conduct rather than about cleverness. Reason, in the Quran's usage, shows itself in how a person lives.",
  "20": "\"˹They are˺ those who honour Allah's covenant, never breaking the pledge\" begins the description of the people of reason mentioned in the previous verse, and the first quality named is faithfulness to a commitment.\n\nThe Arabic indicates fulfilling something completely, giving it its full due rather than partially keeping it.\n\nCommentators have explained Allah's covenant as covering several layers. There is the primordial covenant the Quran describes in Surah al-A'raf, where humanity was made to testify that Allah is their Lord. There is the commitment a person makes by accepting faith, agreeing to worship Him alone and follow His guidance. And there are the specific obligations that follow: prayer, honesty, the rights of others.\n\n\"Never breaking the pledge\" widens it. The Arabic word here covers firm agreements generally, which includes promises made to people as well as commitments made to Allah.\n\nThat inclusion is important, and it reflects how Islam treats the two as connected. Breaking a contract with a business partner, failing to keep a promise to a child, and abandoning a religious obligation belong to the same moral category. The Prophet listed breaking a trust among the signs of hypocrisy in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim, alongside lying and breaking promises.\n\nIt is striking that this appears first in a list describing people of reason. One might expect the description to begin with belief or worship. It begins with reliability.\n\nThere is a logic in that placement. A person whose word means nothing cannot be trusted in anything else, and religion built on a foundation of broken commitments is a performance rather than a faith.\n\nThe description that follows over the next verses continues in the same direction: maintaining relationships, standing in awe of Allah, patience, prayer, giving, and returning good for evil. Reason, in this chapter's account, produces a particular kind of person rather than a particular set of arguments.",
  "21": "\"And those who maintain whatever ˹ties˺ Allah has ordered to be maintained\" continues the description, and it moves from keeping promises to keeping connections.\n\nThe Arabic indicates joining something that Allah has commanded to be joined, and commentators have understood it broadly. It certainly includes the bonds of kinship, which Islam treats with great seriousness. It also includes the connection between a person and his Lord, and the ties of brotherhood among believers.\n\nThe obligation to maintain family ties is emphasised strongly in the Sunnah. The Prophet said, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim, that whoever wishes for expanded provision and a longer life should maintain his family ties, and he warned that the one who cuts them off will not enter Paradise.\n\nWhat makes this instruction demanding is that these relationships are usually the hardest to maintain. A person chooses his friends and can leave them; family is given, and often includes people who are difficult, unfair, or hurtful. The Prophet addressed exactly this, saying that the one who truly maintains ties is not the one who reciprocates, but the one who restores the connection when it is cut.\n\n\"Stand in awe of their Lord\" names the inner state accompanying this conduct. The Arabic indicates fear mixed with reverence, the same word used earlier in this chapter for the angels.\n\n\"And fear strict judgment.\" adds a specific concern: the severity of the reckoning. The Arabic phrase indicates an examination conducted in detail, where everything is looked at closely.\n\nThis is worth pausing over. These are the people just described as fulfilling covenants and maintaining ties, and they are the ones afraid of the reckoning. Those with the best record are the most concerned about it.\n\nThat is a recurring pattern in the Quran and the Sunnah. Confidence about one's own standing tends to accompany carelessness, and fear of the reckoning tends to accompany the conduct that makes it survivable.",
  "22": "\"And ˹they are˺ those who endure patiently, seeking their Lord's pleasure\" continues the portrait, and it specifies not just the action but the motive behind it.\n\nPatience alone is not the qualification. People endure hardship for many reasons: because they have no alternative, to preserve their reputation, or out of sheer stubbornness. What is described here is endurance undertaken for one purpose, seeking Allah's face, which the Arabic phrase indicates.\n\n\"Establish prayer\" is the next quality, and the verb matters. Not merely performing it, but establishing it: keeping it regular, complete, and central.\n\n\"Donate from what We have provided for them—secretly and openly\" names giving, and it includes both forms deliberately. Secret charity protects the recipient's dignity and the giver's sincerity, and the Prophet named among those shaded on the Day of Judgment a man whose left hand does not know what his right hand gave. Open giving encourages others and establishes the practice publicly. Both have their place, and a mature believer knows which is appropriate.\n\nNotice the phrase \"from what We have provided for them.\" What is given was never theirs to begin with, which removes any grounds for feeling generous about it.\n\n\"And respond to evil with good.\" is the hardest item on the list.\n\nThe Arabic indicates repelling something bad by means of something good. This is not passive tolerance; it is an active response of a different kind than the one received. Surah Fussilat expands on it, instructing that the good deed and the evil deed are not equal, and that repelling evil with what is better can turn an enemy into a close friend.\n\nThe chapter on Joseph had just illustrated this in detail. A man forgave brothers who had destroyed his childhood, and prayed for them.\n\n\"It is they who will have the ultimate abode\" closes with the outcome, and the next verses describe it.",
  "23": "\"The Gardens of Eternity, which they will enter along with the righteous among their parents, spouses, and descendants.\" names the reward and adds a detail that transforms it.\n\nThey will not enter alone. The people they loved most in this world will be with them.\n\nThis addresses something that would otherwise cast a shadow over any description of Paradise. A perfect place occupied without one's family would not feel perfect to most people. The verse answers that concern directly, and the Quran states elsewhere in Surah at-Tur that those who believed and whose descendants followed them in faith will be joined with their offspring, without any reduction in the parents' own reward.\n\nThe word \"righteous\" is the necessary qualification. The connection is not automatic, and the preceding chapters have shown this repeatedly. Noah's son drowned. Lot's wife was left behind. Family membership transfers nothing by itself.\n\nWhat the verse describes is a reunion of those who each qualified, and this gives a strong motive for taking one's household seriously. Encouraging a spouse toward prayer, teaching children, and being patient with elderly parents are not merely social duties. They are efforts to secure the company you want in the place you are hoping to reach.\n\n\"And the angels will enter upon them from every gate\" describes the welcome. Angels arrive from every direction, which suggests both the abundance of the honour and the completeness of it.\n\nThe image is one of hospitality on an enormous scale. Guests are received, greeted, and attended to by beings whose usual role in this world involves recording deeds and delivering revelation.\n\nThere is a contrast worth noticing with the earlier description of the other group, who face a strict reckoning and an evil resting place. Here the arrival is a welcome rather than an interrogation.\n\nWhat the angels say as they enter is recorded in the next verse, and it is one of the shortest and warmest greetings in the Quran.",
  "24": "\"\"Peace be upon you for your perseverance.\"\" is the greeting the angels bring, and every word of it repays attention.\n\nThe greeting itself is peace, which in Arabic indicates safety, security, and freedom from all harm. For people who have just come through the reckoning, this is the news that matters most. Whatever they feared is over.\n\nMuslims exchange this greeting daily, and the Quran describes it as the greeting of Paradise. What begins as an ordinary courtesy between believers in this world turns out to be the language of the next.\n\n\"For your perseverance\" is the reason given, and the choice of quality is remarkable.\n\nOf everything the previous verses listed, honouring covenants, maintaining ties, standing in awe, establishing prayer, giving in charity, and repelling evil with good, the angels name patience as the reason for the welcome.\n\nCommentators have explained this by noting that patience underlies all the others. Keeping a promise when it becomes costly requires patience. Maintaining ties with difficult relatives requires patience. Praying regularly across decades requires patience. Giving from limited means requires patience. Responding to an insult with kindness requires more patience than anything else on the list.\n\nThe Arabic word for it, \"sabr,\" means holding oneself firm, and it covers three areas: bearing hardship, restraining oneself from what is forbidden, and persisting in obedience. All three appear in the description of these people.\n\nSurah az-Zumar states that the patient will be given their reward without measure, which is the only quality the Quran describes in those terms.\n\n\"How excellent is the ultimate abode!\" closes the greeting with an exclamation.\n\nThe phrase deliberately mirrors what was said about the other group, whose resting place was called evil. The same structure, the opposite verdict, and the difference between them lies in a lifetime of holding steady when it would have been easier not to.",
  "25": "\"And those who violate Allah's covenant after it has been affirmed\" turns to the opposite group, and the description is deliberately built as a mirror of the one just given.\n\nWhere the people of reason honoured the covenant, these break it. The wording notes that the breaking comes after affirmation, which makes it worse. This is not a person who never agreed; it is someone who committed and then abandoned the commitment.\n\n\"Break whatever ˹ties˺ Allah has ordered to be maintained\" reverses the second quality in the same way. The connections that were to be joined are severed: family bonds cut, the relationship with Allah neglected, the community of believers abandoned.\n\nIslamic teaching treats the cutting of kinship ties with real severity, and the Prophet stated in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim that the one who severs them will not enter Paradise.\n\n\"And spread corruption in the land\" is the third element, and it moves from personal failure to public harm. The Arabic word covers ruining what is sound, whether through injustice, dishonesty, violence, or the destruction of trust between people.\n\nThe three form a sequence with an internal logic. A person who breaks his commitments to Allah loses the anchor for his obligations to others. Once relationships are treated as disposable, harming the wider society follows naturally, since the restraints that held him were all connected to the first commitment.\n\n\"It is they who will be condemned\" states the outcome. The Arabic indicates being cursed, driven far from Allah's mercy, which is precisely the opposite of the peace the angels brought to the other group.\n\n\"And will have the worst abode.\" completes the mirror. One group hears how excellent their final home is; this group receives the reverse verdict in matching words.\n\nThe parallel construction across these verses is doing something deliberate. Two ways of living are set side by side, item by item, so that a reader can locate himself in one column or the other without needing anyone to explain it.",
  "26": "\"Allah gives abundant or limited provisions to whoever He wills.\" addresses a question that troubles people in every generation, and it does so without softening the difficulty.\n\nWealth is distributed unevenly, and the verse attributes that distribution directly to Allah's will. The Arabic indicates expanding provision for some and restricting it for others.\n\nThis is not a statement that the rich are favoured and the poor are punished. The Quran and the Sunnah rule that reading out entirely. Prophets lived in poverty, and some of the worst opponents of the message were the wealthiest men in Mecca. Both conditions are tests, and each carries its own difficulty: wealth tests gratitude and restraint, while limitation tests patience and trust.\n\n\"And the disbelievers become prideful of ˹the pleasures of˺ this worldly life.\" describes the reaction that turns a test into a failure. The Arabic indicates rejoicing in something with arrogance, taking it as evidence of one's own standing.\n\nThe mistake is treating provision as a verdict. A person who has been given much concludes that he must be approved of, and a person given little concludes the opposite. Neither conclusion follows.\n\n\"But the life of this world, compared to the Hereafter, is nothing but a fleeting enjoyment.\" puts the whole matter in proportion. The Arabic word for enjoyment indicates something used temporarily and left behind, like provisions taken for a journey.\n\nThe Prophet illustrated this comparison in a hadith recorded by Muslim, telling his companions to consider what a person's finger brings back after dipping it into the sea. What remains on the finger, compared to the ocean, is what this world amounts to beside the next.\n\nThat image is worth holding on to when the distribution of wealth in this life seems unjust. The differences that occupy so much human attention are differences within a drop.",
  "27": "\"The disbelievers say, \"If only a sign could be sent down to him from his Lord.\"\" repeats a demand recorded earlier in this same chapter, and its repetition is itself informative. They kept asking.\n\nThe answer given here is different from the earlier one, and it goes deeper. Previously the reply was that the Prophet is only a warner. Now the response addresses the real problem.\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Indeed, Allah leaves to stray whoever He wills, and guides to Himself whoever turns to Him\"\" identifies where guidance actually comes from, and it explains why more signs would not help.\n\nThe second half of the sentence is the key, and it must be read carefully to avoid a serious misunderstanding. Allah guides to Himself whoever turns to Him. The Arabic word \"anaba\" indicates turning back, returning, orienting oneself toward something.\n\nThis is not arbitrary. Guidance is described as coming to the person who turns, which means the turning is the condition. A person who orients himself toward Allah, even hesitantly, finds the way opened. A person who has made up his mind against it is left with the choice he made, which is what the first half of the sentence describes.\n\nThe Quran states this pattern in several places, including the promise in Surah al-Ankabut that those who strive in His cause will be guided to His paths.\n\nApplied to the demand for a sign, the answer becomes clear. Signs do not create the turning; they are received by someone who has already turned. The preceding chapters gave the evidence for this repeatedly, from Pharaoh who saw everything and refused, to those described as not believing even if every sign came to them.\n\nWhat the verse offers instead of a spectacular miracle is something more accessible. Turn, and you will be guided. That door is open to anyone, in any condition, at any time.",
  "28": "\"Those who believe and whose hearts find comfort in the remembrance of Allah.\" continues describing the people mentioned in the previous verse, and it names the effect that belief has on the inner life.\n\nThe Arabic word for this comfort, \"itmi'nan,\" indicates settling down, becoming still and secure. It describes a heart that has stopped churning.\n\nThat is a significant claim, and it is worth examining rather than accepting as a pleasant phrase. Human beings are restless by nature. People pursue money, status, relationships, and distraction, and the pursuit rarely produces the settled state they imagined. Each achievement is followed by a new object of anxiety.\n\nThe verse locates the resolution in remembrance of Allah. The Arabic \"dhikr\" covers reciting the Quran, praising Allah, praying, and simply keeping Him in mind through the day.\n\n\"Surely in the remembrance of Allah do hearts find comfort.\" repeats the statement as a general principle, which gives it the force of a rule rather than a description of a few people.\n\nThis is one of the most quoted verses in the Quran, and it is worth noting what it does not promise. It does not say that remembrance removes hardship, prevents illness, or resolves difficulties. It says the heart settles. Jacob wept until he lost his sight, and his statement that he complained only to Allah shows a man whose grief was real and whose heart was still anchored.\n\nThere is a practical test in this that anyone can apply. Periods of neglecting prayer and remembrance tend to coincide with a particular kind of internal unease, which is often blamed on circumstances. Returning to regular remembrance frequently produces a steadiness that the circumstances do not explain.\n\nThe Prophet described the connection in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, comparing the one who remembers his Lord to the living and the one who does not to the dead.",
  "29": "\"Those who believe and do good, for them will be bliss and an honourable destination.\" closes the description of this group with a promise, and the pairing at the start is the Quran's standard formula.\n\nBelief and good deeds appear together throughout the Quran, and the combination is not decorative. Belief without action is a claim; action without belief lacks the foundation that gives it meaning. The two are presented as a single condition rather than as alternatives.\n\nThe word rendered as bliss is \"tuba,\" and commentators have discussed its meaning with genuine variety. Its root relates to what is good and pleasant, and many explained it as goodness, happiness, and a fine state. Some early scholars reported that it refers to a tree in Paradise so vast that a rider would travel in its shade for a hundred years, and reports of this exist in the hadith literature. Others understood it as an expression meaning that a good outcome and delight are theirs.\n\nThe range of these explanations is worth acknowledging honestly rather than presenting one as certain. What all of them share is a sense of complete well-being.\n\n\"And an honourable destination.\" adds the second half. The Arabic indicates a fine place of return, a good end to the journey.\n\nThe word for destination is significant in a chapter that has spent so much time on outcomes. Everything in these verses has concerned where things end: the foam that vanishes and the water that remains, the strict reckoning and the peaceful welcome, the worst abode and the excellent one.\n\nHuman beings naturally judge situations by how they look at the moment. The Quran keeps insisting on judging by where they arrive, which is a discipline that requires effort precisely because the present is so much more vivid than the future.\n\nFor the small community hearing this in Mecca, poor and harassed, the promise of an honourable destination was addressing a situation in which the present offered them very little.",
  "30": "\"And so We have sent you ˹O Prophet˺ to a community, like ˹We did with˺ earlier communities\" places the Prophet's mission in a line rather than presenting it as something unprecedented.\n\nThis served two purposes for the original audience. It answered those who found the whole idea strange, by pointing out that this is how Allah has always dealt with people. And it reassured the Prophet, who was facing exactly what earlier messengers faced.\n\n\"So that you may recite to them what We have revealed to you.\" states his task precisely. Recite what has been revealed. Not what would be popular, not what would be easier to accept, and not what he might have preferred to say.\n\n\"Yet they deny the Most Compassionate.\" records their response, and the name chosen is deliberate.\n\nThe Arabic \"ar-Rahman\" indicates the vast, all-encompassing mercy that reaches every creature. It is reported that the Meccans objected to this particular name and claimed not to recognise it, which the Quran mentions elsewhere. There is a bitter irony in denying the One whose defining description is mercy, while living entirely on that mercy from one breath to the next.\n\n\"Say, \"He is my Lord! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him.\"\" is the reply the Prophet is instructed to give, and its tone is not defensive. He does not argue about the name. He states his position.\n\n\"In Him I put my trust, and to Him I turn ˹in repentance˺.\" completes the declaration with two attitudes that appeared throughout the preceding chapters. Reliance handles what lies ahead; turning back handles what has passed.\n\nThe second word here is the same one used a few verses earlier, where guidance was promised to whoever turns to Allah. The Prophet is described as doing exactly what the guided are described as doing.\n\nThere is a completeness in this short reply. It names his Lord, denies every rival, and states where he places his confidence and where he directs his return.",
  "31": "\"If there were a recitation that could cause mountains to move, or the earth to split, or the dead to speak, ˹it would have been this Quran˺.\" answers the demand for miracles by conceding the premise and then showing why it changes nothing.\n\nThe Arabic sentence is left incomplete in a way that is powerful in the original, and the translation supplies the implied conclusion. If any recitation could do such things, it would be this one.\n\nThree miracles are named, and they are the greatest imaginable: mountains moved, the earth torn open, the dead brought back to speak. These are far beyond the specific requests the Meccans had made.\n\n\"But all matters are by Allah's Will.\" explains why they were not produced. The decision is not the Prophet's, and it is not made according to what would be most impressive.\n\n\"Have the believers not yet realized that had Allah willed, He could have guided all of humanity?\" turns to address the believers themselves, which is an unexpected shift.\n\nThey too had been hoping for a decisive sign that would settle everything. The verse reminds them that universal guidance was always within Allah's power, and that He chose a world in which people are tested rather than compelled. Surah Yunus put the same point to the Prophet directly, asking whether he would force people to become believers.\n\n\"And disasters will continue to afflict the disbelievers or strike close to their homes for their misdeeds\" describes what will happen instead of the requested miracle. Repeated calamities, some reaching them and some landing nearby, serve as warnings within history.\n\nCommentators connected this to the defeats and losses the Meccan opposition suffered in the years that followed.\n\n\"Until Allah's promise comes to pass. Surely Allah never fails in His promise.\" closes with the guarantee. The timing belongs to Him, and the outcome is not in question.",
  "32": "\"Other messengers had already been ridiculed before you\" is offered to the Prophet as consolation, and its method is worth noticing. He is not told that the mockery will stop, and he is not told to ignore it. He is told it has always happened.\n\nThe Quran repeats this consolation in several places, and Surah al-An'am adds that those who mocked were eventually surrounded by the very thing they ridiculed.\n\nRidicule is a particularly effective weapon against a message, which is why it appears so consistently. It requires no argument, no evidence, and no risk. A joke can dismiss a claim that a person cannot actually answer, and it allows an audience to feel superior without thinking.\n\nNoah was mocked while building his ship. Hud was called mad. Salih was told his people had once had high hopes for him. Shu'aib was sarcastically called forbearing and sensible. The Prophet Muhammad was called a poet, a soothsayer, and a man possessed.\n\n\"But I delayed the disbelievers ˹for a while˺ then seized them.\" describes what followed. The Arabic indicates giving them respite, extending their time.\n\nThe delay is the part that the mockers always misread. They took it as evidence that nothing was coming, when it was simply the term they had been granted. Surah Hud made this explicit, describing people who asked what was holding the punishment back.\n\n\"And how ˹horrible˺ was My punishment!\" closes the verse as an exclamation rather than a description, leaving the seizure undescribed. The preceding chapters had already described it in detail: the flood, the wind, the blast, the overturned cities.\n\nFor a man in Mecca being laughed at in the street, the verse offers a particular kind of steadiness. The laughter is expected, the delay is deliberate, and the outcome has a long historical record behind it.",
  "33": "\"Is ˹there any equal to˺ the Vigilant One Who knows what every soul commits?\" is a question that answers itself, and it exposes the absurdity of what the idol worshippers were doing.\n\nThe Arabic name here, \"Qa'im,\" indicates the One who stands over every soul, watching, sustaining, and maintaining it. Nothing a person earns escapes that oversight.\n\nSetting anything alongside such a Being is not a small theological error. It is placing an object that cannot see, hear, or move beside the One who is aware of every deed of every creature.\n\n\"Yet the pagans have associated others with Allah ˹in worship˺.\" states what they did anyway.\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Name them!\"\" is a challenge with a sharp edge. Tell us who these partners actually are. Describe them. State what they have done, what they created, what they control.\n\nThe demand exposes the emptiness of the claim, because there was nothing to say. They could produce names of stones and inherited titles, and nothing behind them.\n\n\"Or do you ˹mean to˺ inform Him of something He does not know on the earth?\" pushes further with heavy irony. If these partners exist and Allah has not mentioned them, are you claiming to have information He lacks?\n\n\"Or are these ˹gods˺ just empty words?\" gives the real answer. The Arabic indicates outward speech with no substance, talk that refers to nothing. Joseph had said the same thing to two prisoners: these are mere names you and your forefathers invented.\n\n\"In fact, the disbelievers' falsehood has been made so appealing to them that they have been turned away from the Path.\" explains how such an empty position is held so firmly. Falsehood was made attractive, and attraction is what sustains it rather than evidence.\n\n\"And whoever Allah leaves to stray will be left with no guide.\" closes the verse, and the preceding verses have already given the condition: guidance comes to whoever turns.",
  "34": "\"For them is punishment in this worldly life\" continues the description of those left to stray, and it begins with consequences that arrive before death.\n\nCommentators have understood this worldly punishment in several ways. It includes the defeats, losses, and calamities that the previous verses mentioned striking near their homes. It also includes something less visible: the inner condition of a person cut off from guidance, described elsewhere in the Quran as a constricted life, tight and difficult regardless of outward comfort.\n\nThat second dimension is worth taking seriously. Punishment is not always dramatic. A heart with no anchor, no answer to the question of purpose, and no peace in remembrance carries a weight that money does not lift.\n\n\"But the punishment of the Hereafter is truly far worse.\" places the two in proportion. Whatever difficulty arrives here is temporary and limited; what follows is neither.\n\nThe comparison serves a purpose beyond warning. It quietly prevents a misreading of worldly suffering, since believers also experience hardship, and the Quran never treats difficulty in this life as proof of divine displeasure. What distinguishes the two groups is where things end.\n\n\"And none can shield them from Allah.\" closes the verse by removing the possibility of protection. The Arabic indicates one who guards or defends someone from something.\n\nIn the tribal world of the audience, protection was everything. A man's safety depended on who stood behind him, and the early Muslims in Mecca suffered most when they had no clan to defend them. The verse tells those who trusted in such arrangements that in this matter nobody is standing behind them.\n\nThis connects to what the surrounding chapters have said repeatedly. Wealth does not shield, allies do not shield, and the idols invoked for protection cannot protect themselves. The only shelter available is the one being offered by the message they were refusing, which is the irony at the centre of the whole discussion.",
  "35": "\"The description of the Paradise promised to the righteous is that under it rivers flow\" begins one of the Quran's brief portraits of the reward, and it opens with water.\n\nFor an audience in a desert peninsula, flowing water was the definition of abundance. Rivers meant permanent life, not the temporary relief of a rain shower that dries within days. The image would have landed with particular force on people whose survival depended on wells and seasonal wadis.\n\n\"Eternal is its fruit as well as its shade.\" names the two features that matter most in that climate, and attaches permanence to both.\n\nFruit in this world is seasonal. A tree gives for a few weeks and then gives nothing, and every harvest is followed by a period of waiting. The verse removes that limitation entirely.\n\nShade in a hot land is not a luxury but a necessity, and it shifts constantly as the sun moves. Permanent shade means rest that does not have to be chased.\n\nThere is something worth noticing about what the description emphasises. It is not the intensity of pleasure but its permanence. This addresses the flaw that runs through every good thing in this life: enjoyment shadowed by the knowledge that it will end. A meal finishes, health declines, and even great happiness carries an awareness of its own limit.\n\n\"That is the ˹ultimate˺ outcome for the righteous.\" uses a word indicating the final result, what things come to after everything has played out. This chapter has been insisting on judging by outcomes rather than by present appearances, and the word appears here deliberately.\n\n\"But the outcome for the disbelievers is the Fire!\" completes the contrast in a single short line.\n\nThe brevity is effective. Paradise receives a description; the alternative receives one word. There is nothing to describe about it that would take longer.",
  "36": "\"˹The believers among˺ those who were given the Scripture rejoice at what has been revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺\" records a response that the Meccans would not have expected.\n\nJews and Christians of the region possessed earlier revelation, and some among them recognised what was being recited as consistent with what they already knew. Their reaction was joy rather than resistance. The Quran mentions such people in several places, describing those who, when the revelation is recited to them, fall down in prostration, and Surah Ali Imran notes that among the People of the Book is an upright community reciting Allah's verses through the night.\n\nThis detail mattered for the Prophet's argument in Mecca. The people best positioned to detect a forgery, those familiar with earlier scripture, included some who welcomed it.\n\n\"While some ˹disbelieving˺ factions deny a portion of it.\" gives the other side honestly. Not everyone accepted, and among the various groups there were those who rejected parts of what was revealed.\n\nThe phrase \"a portion of it\" is precise. Their rejection was often selective, accepting what matched their existing positions and refusing what corrected them, which is a recognisable pattern in every religious community.\n\n\"Say, \"I have only been commanded to worship Allah, associating none with Him ˹in worship˺.\"\" is the Prophet's response to the whole situation, and it refuses to be drawn into the disputes between factions.\n\nHe states his instruction rather than debating their objections. What he was commanded is simple and non-negotiable: worship Allah, associate nothing with Him.\n\n\"To Him I invite ˹all˺, and to Him is my return.\" completes the declaration. His invitation is to Allah rather than to a party, a tribe, or himself, and his own destination is the same one he is calling others toward.\n\nThere is a discipline in this reply worth learning from. Faced with mixed reactions, partial acceptance, and organised opposition, he restates the core rather than fragmenting into a hundred arguments.",
  "37": "\"And so We have revealed it as an authority in Arabic.\" describes the Quran with a word that carries weight. The Arabic \"hukm\" indicates a decisive judgement, a ruling that settles matters rather than merely offering an opinion.\n\nThis follows directly from the previous verse, where various groups accepted or rejected different portions. In a situation of competing claims, a final authority is needed, and the Quran presents itself as exactly that.\n\nThe mention of Arabic serves the purpose stated in the preceding chapter: revelation comes in the language of its first audience so that they can understand it without an intermediary. A ruling nobody can read settles nothing.\n\n\"And if you were to follow their desires after ˹all˺ the knowledge that has come to you, there would be none to protect or shield you from Allah.\" is a warning addressed to the Prophet, and it is severe.\n\nVerses of this kind appear several times in the Quran, and they are not accusations. The Prophet never followed their desires, and the Quran itself testifies to his character. The purpose is to show the seriousness of the matter by addressing the most protected person of all. If such a warning is directed at him, nobody listening can imagine himself exempt.\n\nThe wording identifies the specific danger: following desires after knowledge has arrived. This is different from making an honest mistake. It describes abandoning what one knows to be true because someone else wants something different.\n\nThe pressure in Mecca took exactly this form. The Quraysh repeatedly proposed compromises, offering acceptance in exchange for softening the message, dropping the criticism of their gods, or dismissing the poor believers from his circle. Every proposal was a request to follow their desires.\n\nThe verse closes the door completely, and it does so by removing any imagined safety net. Nobody protects a person from Allah, which is the same point the chapter made about the disbelievers a few verses earlier.",
  "38": "\"We have certainly sent messengers before you ˹O Prophet˺ and blessed them with wives and offspring.\" answers an objection the Meccans raised about the Prophet's ordinary human life.\n\nThey expected a messenger from God to be something other than a man with a household. Marriage, children, and family responsibilities seemed to them incompatible with prophethood.\n\nThe verse points out that this was always the pattern. Noah had a family, Abraham had sons, Jacob had twelve, Moses married and had children, and David and Solomon had households. Prophets were not celibate ascetics standing apart from human life; they lived inside it.\n\nThis has practical significance beyond answering the objection. A messenger who does not marry cannot demonstrate how a husband should behave. One without children cannot show how a father should raise them. The Prophet's family life is a substantial part of what Muslims learn from, and his conduct with his wives and children is recorded in detail precisely because it was meant to be followed.\n\n\"It was not for any messenger to bring a sign without Allah's permission.\" addresses the second objection, which was the demand for miracles.\n\nNo prophet ever produced a sign on his own initiative. Moses did not decide when the staff would become a serpent, and Jesus did not heal at will independently of Allah's permission. Miracles came when Allah sent them, for His purposes, not as demonstrations arranged on request.\n\nThis settles the Meccan demand definitively. Asking the Prophet for a sign was asking him for something that was never in any messenger's hands.\n\n\"Every destined matter has a ˹set˺ time.\" closes the verse with the principle that governs it. The Arabic indicates a written, recorded term.\n\nSigns, punishments, victories, and every other decreed event has an appointed moment. Demanding that something arrive earlier misunderstands how the arrangement works, which is a theme running through these chapters.",
  "39": "\"Allah eliminates and confirms what He wills, and with Him is the Master Record.\" is one of the most discussed verses in the Quran, and it deals with the relationship between what is written and what happens.\n\nThe Arabic indicates erasing something and establishing something, and scholars have examined it carefully because it appears to describe change in what has been decreed.\n\nThe explanation generally given distinguishes between two levels. There is what the verse calls the Master Record, the Arabic phrase indicating the mother of the Book, the original and complete record with Allah in which everything is fixed and nothing changes. And there is what is recorded at the level of angels and immediate circumstances, where erasure and confirmation occur.\n\nUnderstood this way, the verse does not describe Allah changing His mind, which is impossible for the One whose knowledge is complete. It describes events unfolding in ways that appear as change to us, while the final outcome was always known.\n\nThis has a practical dimension that the Sunnah makes explicit. The Prophet said, in a hadith recorded by at-Tirmidhi, that nothing repels the decree except supplication, and that righteousness can extend a person's life. Reports also mention that maintaining family ties increases provision and lifespan.\n\nThese statements sit comfortably with this verse. A person's prayer may be the very means by which something is changed, and that means was itself part of what was known and written. Supplication is not an attempt to overturn the decree; it is part of it.\n\nThe practical effect is important. A believer is never in a position where effort and prayer are pointless because everything is already fixed. The Quran and the Sunnah consistently urge both, which would be senseless if nothing could ever be affected.\n\nWhat the verse ultimately establishes is that the pen is in Allah's hand, and the original record is with Him.",
  "40": "\"Whether We show you ˹O Prophet˺ some of what We threaten them with, or cause you to die ˹before that˺\" addresses a question that must have weighed on him. Would he live to see the outcome of his mission?\n\nBoth possibilities are named without preference, and the verse treats them as equally acceptable. This is a striking thing to say to a man who had spent years being mocked and threatened.\n\nThe word \"some\" is worth noting. Even what a prophet might witness is only a portion of what awaits, since the greater reckoning lies beyond this life entirely.\n\nAs it happened, he did see part of it. The Meccan leaders who had persecuted his companions were defeated at Badr within a few years of the migration, and he eventually entered Mecca peacefully as its people opened the gates.\n\n\"Your duty is only to deliver ˹the message˺.\" states his responsibility with complete clarity. The Arabic word indicates conveying something so that it reaches its destination.\n\nThat is the whole of it. Not producing belief, not securing acceptance, not guaranteeing results. Delivery.\n\nThis limit appears repeatedly in these chapters, and it is one of the most practically useful ideas in the Quran for anyone who cares about another person's guidance. A parent who has taught, explained, and modelled has done the work. A friend who has spoken honestly has done the work. What happens next is outside the job description.\n\n\"Judgment is for Us.\" names who handles the rest. The Arabic indicates the reckoning, the calling to account.\n\nThe division is complete: conveying belongs to the messenger, reckoning belongs to Allah. Confusing the two is the source of a great deal of unnecessary distress, and also of a great deal of harm, since people who take the second role upon themselves tend toward force and condemnation.\n\nThe Prophet was told plainly that neither belonged to him.",
  "41": "\"Do they not see that We gradually reduce their land from its borders?\" points to something happening in front of the Meccans while they argued.\n\nCommentators have understood this in several complementary ways. Many read it as referring to the steady expansion of Islam, with territory around Mecca coming under the influence of the new community while the Quraysh watched their sphere shrink. Others understood it more generally, as the reduction of a land through the loss of its people, its scholars, and its best inhabitants over time.\n\nBoth readings describe a gradual process rather than a sudden collapse, which is what makes the verse effective. The Meccans had demanded dramatic signs. What they were being given was slow, unmistakable, and visible to anyone paying attention.\n\nThere is something instructive in this about how change usually arrives. Nations rarely fall in a single day. Edges erode, influence contracts, and confidence continues long after the ground has begun to shift. The people inside the process are typically the last to recognise it.\n\n\"Allah decides—none can reverse His decision.\" states why the process could not be stopped. The Arabic indicates that no one follows up His ruling to overturn it, the way a higher court might reverse a lower one. There is no appeal above this.\n\n\"And He is swift in reckoning.\" closes the verse with a name that has a double edge in this context.\n\nIt means the accounting is not delayed by difficulty, since Allah does not need time to examine records or weigh evidence. It also implies that what seems like a long delay from a human standpoint is nothing at all.\n\nFor people demanding to know when the promised outcome would arrive, this was the answer. The reckoning is not slow; their perception of time is.\n\nThe chapter has already supplied the other half of this picture. It stated earlier that 'Allah would never change a people's state of favour until they change their own state of faith.' A shrinking border was not fate acting blindly; it tracked a people's own choices, which is what makes the process just as well as gradual.",
  "42": "\"Those ˹disbelievers˺ before them ˹secretly˺ planned, but Allah has the ultimate plan.\" places the Meccan opposition in a long line of failed schemes.\n\nThe Arabic word \"makr\" indicates planning carried out quietly, working toward a result by indirect means. Applied to human beings it usually carries the sense of plotting.\n\nEvery community described in the preceding chapters attempted something of this kind. Noah's people plotted against him and were invited to gather their schemes openly. Joseph's brothers planned in private and executed their scheme successfully. Pharaoh's court organised a public contest to destroy Moses. The Meccans would later plot to kill the Prophet on the night of his migration.\n\n\"But Allah has the ultimate plan\" states the outcome of all of it. When the word is used of Allah, it does not carry the sense of deceit. It indicates that His arrangement encompasses and overturns theirs, so that their plotting serves an end they never intended.\n\nJoseph's story demonstrated this at length. Ten men schemed to remove a boy, and every step of their plan carried him toward the position his dream had promised.\n\n\"He knows what every soul commits.\" explains why no plot can succeed. Secrecy is the essential ingredient of scheming, and secrecy does not exist. This chapter established the point earlier, noting that speaking quietly or openly, hiding in the night or moving in daylight, are all the same to Him.\n\n\"And the disbelievers will soon know who will have the ultimate outcome.\" closes with the word for the final result that has recurred throughout this chapter.\n\nThe question of who ends well cannot be settled in the middle of the story, which is exactly the difficulty for people living inside it. The verse does not argue about it. It simply says they will find out.\n\nA few verses earlier, the Prophet was told that his duty was only to deliver the message, and that judgment belongs to Allah. The scheming around him was therefore not his to outplot; this verse quietly tells him whose problem it was.",
  "43": "\"The disbelievers say, \"You ˹Muḥammad˺ are no messenger.\"\" states the accusation in its simplest form, stripped of the various explanations they had offered.\n\nThey had called him a poet, a soothsayer, a madman, and a fabricator. Behind all of it lay this single denial: he was not sent.\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Allah is sufficient as a Witness between me and you\"\" is the response, and its confidence is remarkable in its restraint.\n\nHe does not list his evidence, recount his character, or point to the Quran's challenge. He calls a witness, and the witness is Allah.\n\nCalling Allah as witness is the strongest testimony available, and it is not a rhetorical gesture. If the Prophet were lying about being sent, this would be the gravest possible offence, fabricating against Allah while calling on Allah to confirm the fabrication. No forger makes such a claim casually.\n\nThe Quran mentions this elsewhere with even sharper terms, stating that if he had invented sayings and attributed them to Allah, He would have seized him by the right hand and severed his lifeline.\n\n\"As is whoever has knowledge of the Scripture.\" adds a second category of witness, and commentators have discussed who is meant.\n\nMany understood it as referring to those among the Jews and Christians who genuinely knew their own scriptures and recognised the truth of what was being recited, which connects to the earlier verse in this chapter about those who rejoice at what was revealed. Some early scholars suggested other readings.\n\nThe appeal is significant either way. He is pointing to people outside his own community, with their own religious learning, who had no reason to support him and every social reason not to.\n\nNotice, too, what the denial itself concedes. Calling him no messenger only matters if messengers exist, and the Quraysh never disputed the category; their own history and the scriptures known in the city were full of sent men. This was not scepticism about prophets in general. It was resistance to this particular one, because his message reached the arrangement of power and worship their city was built on.\n\nThe chapter closes here, and its final word is testimony rather than argument."
};
